select amount,CAST(amount AS signed) AS amount_str from sakila.payment;  -- check type casting in mysql 

-- I was doing some debugging on the SQL queries on built-in functions that we learnt on Friday last session and found out that -> MySQL uses implicit type conversion (coercion) to make operands compatible. So like when we perform an arithmetic or bitwise operation on a string column (like a VARCHAR or DECIMAL from the Sakila.film table) then MySQL automatically converts that string to a number. That is the reason it is showing some integer output in double_rate column as you can see in my Screenshot. Also if we take a close look then I see that the answer which is not an exact exponent or say power of the rental_rate field is incorrect and the reason behind this happening is MSQL rule of Leading Digits. So when SQL is parsed from left to right, it by default does the mathematical operation on the very first number it encounters. I thought of sharing this information. If anyone else was able to dig deeper on typecasting from String to integer explicitly then please share & correct me if I am wrong !
